<div style="background-color: #d1e2f1; padding: 15px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Panama Has Delicious Food! – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Planning Meals for My Family</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Panama Has Delicious Food!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Concept: Use visual aids to explain basic concepts. Concept: Connect historical events discussed in class to real-life examples.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can use visual aids to explain basic concepts about food (e.g., points to vegetables and says, "healthy food"). Can connect food topics to historical examples using simple language (e.g., "People ate rice then and now too." while pointing to a rice picture). Can facilitate a discussion on meal planning with peers by asking questions (e.g., "What should we eat?" and pointing to food options).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="background-color: #e6f2f9;">
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and displays a large visual of a "Healthy Plate" with different food groups (vegetables, fruits, grains, protein). The teacher asks, "What food do you see here?" pointing to different items. "Are these foods healthy?" "What do you like for breakfast?" Students respond with simple words or phrases (e.g., "Rice and eggs!", "Yes, healthy!"). The teacher connects this to the project: "Today, we will talk about our healthy recipes!"</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up two flashcards: one of "rice" and one of "beans." The teacher says, "This is rice. This is beans. Rice and beans are delicious and healthy! People ate rice and beans a long time ago and now too." (pointing to a historical picture if available, or just emphasizing 'then and now'). The teacher models explaining the concept of "healthy food" by pointing to a picture of fruits and vegetables and saying clearly, "Fruits and vegetables are healthy food. They are good for us." (Pronunciation of /v/ in "vegetables" and /f/ in "fruit" is explicitly modeled).</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses visuals and simple language.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to an apple) "Is this food healthy or unhealthy?" (Expected answer: Healthy!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a picture of rice and beans) "Did people eat rice and beans only now, or then and now?" (Expected answer: Then and now!)</li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Planning Meals for My Family".</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple illustrated poster or projected slide showing a "Panamanian Meal Plan for a Day," with pictures and short sentences. Example:
                <br><b>Breakfast:</b> Some bread, eggs, and fresh juice.
                <br><b>Lunch:</b> Rice and beans, chicken, and a salad with tomatoes and lettuce.
                <br><b>Snack:</b> Mangos and watermelon.
                <br><b>Dinner:</b> Fish (steamed) and some vegetables.
                <br>A small historical note is included: "People often ate fish and fruits in Panama a long time ago."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will interpret and understand basic information about daily meals and food types, recognizing connections to the theme "Panama Has Delicious Food!".</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher points to the poster and asks, "What do we eat for lunch?" (Gist question)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">True or False: "For breakfast, we eat rice and beans." (Students show T or F cards).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks, "What fruit is for snack?" (Information matching)</li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Using present simple, modal verb 'can', countable/uncountable nouns, food vocabulary (fruits, vegetables, rice, beans, cook, eat, healthy), pronunciation of /v/ and /f/, prefixes/suffixes (-un "uncountable," -ing "cooking"), pragmatic functions for asking/sharing meal ideas.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Sentence Completion:</b> The teacher provides sentences with blanks and picture prompts. Students complete them orally or in notebooks. Examples:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Picture of tomatoes) "Tomatoes are ____ (healthy/unhealthy) vegetables."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Picture of a plate of rice) "I want to eat ____ (some/many) rice."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Picture of a person cooking) "My mom likes ____ (cooking)." (Phonemic awareness: -ing suffix)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Explaining with Sentence Frames:</b> In pairs, students receive flashcards with food items (e.g., mangos, fish, bread) and a simple historical fact (e.g., "People loved mangos a long time ago."). They use sentence frames to explain to their partner, connecting the past and present.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"This is a ____ (mango/fish). It is ____ (sweet/tasty). People ate ____ (mangos/fish) then and now too."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They also practice: "Fruits are ____ (uncountable)." (Phonemic awareness: -un prefix).</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Meal Planning Discussion:</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair gets a set of food picture cards (e.g., rice, beans, chicken, bread, fruits, water). They discuss and plan a simple meal (e.g., lunch) together. One student asks, "What should we eat for lunch?" The other suggests, "We can cook some rice and chicken. And some water." They point to the cards as they speak. The teacher circulates, monitoring use of 'can,' countable/uncountable nouns, and asking about preferences: "What do you like to eat?"</li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Planning Meals for My Family".</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Mini-Presentation / Peer Explanation of Digital Recipe Book entry.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b> Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group has prepared one or more pages for their "Digital Recipe Book" project, featuring a healthy Panamanian meal. They take turns presenting their recipe page to their group members.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One student acts as the "presenter" for their recipe. They use their recipe page (visual aid) to explain the meal, its ingredients, and simple steps. For example: "This is our 'Rice and Beans with Fish' recipe. It's a healthy meal. First, we boil the rice and beans. Next, we fry the fish. We can also eat some fresh vegetables like tomatoes and lettuce."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The presenter also connects to healthy eating: "Fish and vegetables are very healthy food!" and, if relevant, a historical fact: "Panamanian people often ate fish a long time ago."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Other group members listen and facilitate the discussion by asking questions about the meal or ingredients (e.g., "What ingredients do you use?", "Do you like to eat this?", "Is it a healthy snack?"). They can also share their own preferences: "I like to eat some watermelon for snack."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students ensure they use target vocabulary (rice, beans, fish, vegetables, fruits, cook, boil, fry, healthy, some, many) and grammar (present simple, can). The teacher observes and provides support.</li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy (observation checklist, rubric, or peer assessment).</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Peer Assessment Checklist.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> After the mini-presentations, each student uses a simple "Peer Assessment Checklist" to evaluate one group member's presentation of their recipe book page. The checklist includes:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The student used visual aids to explain the meal. (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The student used words like 'healthy', 'rice', 'vegetables'. (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The student tried to connect to history (e.g., "People ate..."). (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The student asked questions like "What should we eat?". (Yes/No)</li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear enough for peers to understand (especially /v/ and /f/).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Incorporates at least 3-4 key words (e.g., rice, beans, healthy, cook, fruit, vegetables).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity in explanation:</b> Successfully explains basic concepts of their meal using the visual aid.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Facilitation of discussion:</b> Asks simple questions or responds to peers about the meal plan.</li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One new thing I can explain about healthy food is...</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can ask my friends: "What do you like to ____?"</li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Was it easy or difficult to explain your recipe to your friends? Why?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What is one healthy meal you want to eat this week?</li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The teacher considers:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in helping students use visuals and simple language to explain food concepts and historical connections? The peer discussions during the Digital Recipe Book presentations were very engaging.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for the next lesson or future mediation tasks? Some students still struggle with sequencing adverbs (first, next) when explaining steps; more practice with sentence frames for instructions is needed. Also, continue reinforcing countable/uncountable nouns.</li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the theme "Panama Has Delicious Food!". The next lesson will transition to a new theme, possibly reviewing key vocabulary from this unit, or introducing new food items and activities related to a different cultural context or activity. The digital recipe book can be a final project to share with other classes or families.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>